WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580
The first film changed whole-chromosome dose.

2
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:07.780
Can a chromosome rearrangement matter when no DNA seems missing?

3
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.960
Follow the same coloured blocks through breakage and meiosis.

4
00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:18.413
A chromosome is more than a counted object.

5
00:00:19.693 --> 00:00:22.853
Its blocks carry genes, and their order provides context.

6
00:00:24.273 --> 00:00:29.013
For any rearrangement, ask what material changed dose, what breakpoint changed

7
00:00:29.013 --> 00:00:31.753
context, and what products meiosis can make.

8
00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:35.873
Remove one block and the chromosome has a deletion.

9
00:00:37.313 --> 00:00:38.933
Its genes now have lower dose.

10
00:00:40.213 --> 00:00:42.553
A missing segment cannot supply its genes.

11
00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:46.773
Copy one block and the chromosome has a duplication.

12
00:00:48.173 --> 00:00:49.853
Those genes now have higher dose.

13
00:00:51.153 --> 00:00:53.613
An extra segment supplies another copy.

14
00:00:54.873 --> 00:00:56.573
The red gauge tracks both changes.

15
00:00:56.573 --> 00:00:57.493
The red gauge tracks both changes.

16
00:00:57.717 --> 00:01:01.417
Now cut out several blocks and return them in reverse order.

17
00:01:02.637 --> 00:01:03.697
That is an inversion.

18
00:01:05.037 --> 00:01:07.717
No block is lost, so dose can stay balanced.

19
00:01:08.937 --> 00:01:11.397
The chromosome count also stays unchanged.

20
00:01:12.457 --> 00:01:16.377
During meiosis, homologous blocks bend into a loop to align.

21
00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:22.790
A reciprocal translocation exchanges segments between nonhomologous chromosomes.

22
00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:29.250
Move a teal end segment from blue to a grey chromosome, and return a grey segment.

23
00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:34.570
Total dose may stay balanced even though both chromosomes have new neighbours.

24
00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:39.410
Balanced dose does not guarantee no biological effect.

25
00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:42.730
A breakpoint can interrupt a gene block.

26
00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.690
It can also place pieces from different genes together.

27
00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.890
The result depends on which sequence meets the breakpoint.

28
00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:55.950
These are changes in gene context, not whole-chromosome count.

29
00:01:57.033 --> 00:01:58.993
The carrier still faces meiosis.

30
00:02:00.573 --> 00:02:04.193
Normal and translocated partners must align matching blocks.

31
00:02:05.533 --> 00:02:07.933
All four chromosomes form a cross-shaped group.

32
00:02:09.193 --> 00:02:14.533
The way this cross separates decides whether each gamete receives one copy of every

33
00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:15.533
block.

34
00:02:15.933 --> 00:02:21.453
Which separation preserves one copy of every coloured block: opposite chromosomes

35
00:02:21.453 --> 00:02:24.913
moving together, or neighbouring chromosomes moving together?

36
00:02:26.193 --> 00:02:27.373
Look at the block ledgers.

37
00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.720
Opposite partners preserve one copy of every block.

38
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.880
This alternate segregation makes normal or balanced gametes.

39
00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.300
Neighbouring partners give each gamete an extra copy of one segment and no copy of

40
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:53.300
another. This adjacent segregation makes unbalanced gametes.

41
00:02:54.183 --> 00:02:55.943
That explains a common puzzle.

42
00:02:57.243 --> 00:03:00.903
A carrier can be healthy because their chromosome set is balanced.

43
00:03:02.103 --> 00:03:04.803
Yet meiosis can produce unbalanced gametes.

44
00:03:06.083 --> 00:03:08.663
Balance must therefore be tested at both levels.

45
00:03:09.963 --> 00:03:15.223
The rearrangement's consequence depends on breakpoint context and segregation, not

46
00:03:15.223 --> 00:03:16.503
chromosome count alone.

47
00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.930
Trace three things after every break.

48
00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:23.150
Ask what material was gained or lost.

49
00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:27.070
Ask which genes were interrupted or newly joined.

50
00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:32.030
Then ask which chromosome combinations can pass through meiosis.

51
00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:38.250
Rearrangements matter through DNA dose, gene context, and meiotic balance.
